WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:04.980
"Spider-Man: Across the Spider-Verse" is&nbsp;
practically bursting with Spider-People,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:08.280
and most of them actually have&nbsp;
significant origins in the comic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:12.600
books. Wondering what those origins are?&nbsp;
We're here to explain. Spoilers Ahead!

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:18.600
"Spider-Man: Across the Spider-Verse" opens with&nbsp;
Earth-65's Spider-Woman, Gwen Stacy, giving the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:23.040
audience a glimpse of her life after the events of&nbsp;
"Spider-Man: Into the Spider-Verse." We learn that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:27.420
this version of Spider-Gwen has an origin story&nbsp;
similar to her comic book counterpart's, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:32.040
occurred in "Edge of Spider-Verse" issue 2. After&nbsp;
receiving powers from the bite of an irradiated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:36.960
spider, she establishes a secret identity and&nbsp;
fights crime as a vigilante. Her best friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:42.360
Peter Parker, transforms himself into the Lizard,&nbsp;
accidentally dying in their ensuing battle. Gwen's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:46.680
father, police officer George Stacy, mistakenly&nbsp;
assumes that Spider-Woman murdered the boy.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:51.480
Frightened and uncertain about her relationships&nbsp;
and future, she jumps at the chance to join&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:55.800
the Spider Society. This sprawling team of&nbsp;
spider-powered superheroes from different Earths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:00.420
have banded together under Earth-928's Miguel&nbsp;
O'Hara to prevent the fabric of the multiverse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.680
from being destroyed by dimension-hopping&nbsp;
anomalies. Along the way, she also discovers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:09.600
the shocking truth about her only other friend,&nbsp;
Miles Morales, the Spider-Man of Earth-1610,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:14.340
and why he can never join the Society. However,&nbsp;
going against the group's directives, she catches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:18.600
up with Miles during a mission. It's an impulsive&nbsp;
choice that leads to disastrous consequences.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:21.540
Gwen eventually finds herself&nbsp;
kicked out of the Society,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:26.400
as Miles is stranded on a hostile, unfamiliar&nbsp;
Earth. However, the drummer who never belonged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:31.080
decides to form her own "band" of old and new&nbsp;
Spider-friends to locate and rescue Miles.

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:35.400
The protagonist of the "Spider-Verse"&nbsp;
trilogy, Miles Morales is the sole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:39.540
Spider-Man of Earth-1610 and operates in&nbsp;
Brooklyn. Over a year has passed since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:42.900
the events of "Into the Spider-Verse,"&nbsp;
with Miles fully embracing his role as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:46.980
his city's super-powered protector, complete&nbsp;
with a new costume that he designed himself.

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:52.200
"My name is Miles Morales. I'm&nbsp;
Brooklyn's one and only Spider-Man."

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:56.040
He crosses paths with the Spot,&nbsp;
an apparent joke of a villain who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:59.400
rapidly transforms into a terrifying&nbsp;
threat over the course of the film.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:04.080
Miles learns about the existence of the Spider&nbsp;
Society following an unexpected visit from his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:09.420
friend, Earth-65's Gwen Stacy who was secretly&nbsp;
sent to his Earth to monitor and detain the Spot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:14.280
Unbeknownst to her, Miles follows her through a&nbsp;
multiversal portal as she tracks down the villain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:19.260
There his actions nearly cause the destruction&nbsp;
of another Spider-Man's world. At the Society's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:24.120
headquarters, he's denied membership to the group,&nbsp;
and discovers that he was the "original anomaly."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:28.680
The spider that bit him was from Earth-42 and&nbsp;
was supposed to bite that world's Spider-person,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:32.880
not him. Worse, the Society tries to&nbsp;
stop him from saving his father's life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:37.200
as the death of someone close to them is a&nbsp;
"canon event" that all Spiders must suffer.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:40.620
Unwilling to accept this, Miles&nbsp;
makes a desperate bid to go home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:44.280
outmaneuvering the entire Society&nbsp;
in the process. However, he lands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:48.660
on Earth-42 instead and is captured by&nbsp;
that world's Prowler: Miles Morales.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:52.980
"Across the Spider-Verse" builds upon the&nbsp;
post-credits scene of its predecessor by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:57.300
formally introducing Miguel O'Hara,&nbsp;
the Spider-Man of Earth-928. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:01.680
Spider-Man's dimension is set in the year&nbsp;
2099, and New York has transformed into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.060
technologically advanced city of Nueva&nbsp;
York. Aided by his holo-assistant Lyla,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:10.560
Miguel forms the Spider Society, taking it&nbsp;
upon himself to maintain order across what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:15.420
he calls the Arachno-Humanoid Poly-Multiverse.&nbsp;
Interestingly, he has some major beef with Miles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:20.520
Morales. He blames Miles for being the very first&nbsp;
multiverse anomaly in "Into the Spider-Verse."

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:24.360
This version of Spider-Man 2099 is&nbsp;
barely recognizable from the original,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:30.720
who first appeared in 1992's "Spider-Man 2099" #1.&nbsp;
The Miguel we see in "Across the Spider-Verse" is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:35.520
an angry, tortured soul traumatized by his&nbsp;
past failings, completely devoid of a sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:39.780
of humor and prone to throwing objects in a&nbsp;
fit of rage. While not an outright villain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:44.820
he antagonizes Miles to a cruel degree. Aside&nbsp;
from revealing that Miles' father is fated to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:48.720
die and prohibiting him from doing anything&nbsp;
about it, Miguel also tells the fledgling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:53.220
superhero that he's nothing more than a mistake&nbsp;
and that he was never meant to be Spider-Man.

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:57.780
By the movie's end, Miguel and his strike&nbsp;
team are hunting Miles across the multiverse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:01.080
and it's clear that this "vampire&nbsp;
ninja" Spider-Man will stop at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:03.540
nothing to keep the Spider-Verse "canon" intact.

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:08.220
The first new Spider-character introduced in&nbsp;
"Spider-Man: Across the Spider-Verse" is Jessica&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:12.900
Drew, the afroed arachnid-themed defender&nbsp;
of a yet-unrevealed Earth designation. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:16.140
tough-as-nails, tough-love-giving,&nbsp;
motorcycle-riding mother-to-be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:20.220
this web-slinging wonder fearlessly jumps&nbsp;
into battle. She instantly makes a strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:24.720
impression on Spider-Gwen, whom she invites to&nbsp;
the Society and takes under her wing as a protégé.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:30.180
The original version of Jessica Drew was&nbsp;
introduced in 1977's "Marvel Spotlight" #32,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:35.220
and she's been given a major revamp in many ways&nbsp;
for her silver screen animation debut. While not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:39.660
much is known about the origin story of Jessica's&nbsp;
"Across the Spider-Verse" iteration, it is clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:44.460
that the Society's de facto leader, Miguel O'Hara,&nbsp;
trusts her considerably, to the point where she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:48.720
basically acts as his second-in-command.&nbsp;
She is also critical of Peter B. Parker's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:53.400
mentoring skills, hardly hesitating to point to&nbsp;
Miles as proof that he is a terrible teacher.

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:57.600
Initially, Jessica is strict towards Gwen,&nbsp;
emphasizing that the latter is her star student&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:02.340
and downright disapproving of her continued&nbsp;
association with Miles. She even does nothing when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:07.200
Miguel expels Gwen from the Society and sends her&nbsp;
home. By the film's end, however, Jessica seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:11.520
proud that Gwen has taken the initiative to search&nbsp;
for Miles herself, without the Society's support.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:16.260
During the sequence where the Spot first&nbsp;
explores the possibilities of his growing powers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:21.780
he takes a quick detour to Earth-13122, the&nbsp;
home of LEGO Spider-Man. This unique take on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:24.780
the web-slinger was completely absent&nbsp;
in the promotional materials leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:27.720
up to the release of "Spider-Man:&nbsp;
Across the Spider-Verse." However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:32.340
his inclusion was revealed during the main&nbsp;
cast's interview with Fandango. Surprisingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:36.240
LEGO Peter Parker plays a much bigger role&nbsp;
than just a "blink and you'll miss it" cameo.

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:40.440
After the black-and-white baddie shows up in the&nbsp;
LEGO universe and destroys a chunk of the Daily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:45.120
Bugle building, we learn that LEGO Peter Parker&nbsp;
witnessed the whole event. Taking a quick break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:49.500
from J. Jonah Jameson's hotheaded hassling, the&nbsp;
mild-mannered minifigure hides in a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:54.360
room and transforms into LEGO Spider-Man. He&nbsp;
activates his Spider Society device and reports&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:58.140
the incident to Miguel O'Hara, who thanks him&nbsp;
and calls him one of their best Spider-Men.

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:02.340
LEGO Spider-Man is a welcome addition&nbsp;
here and also represents a "full-circle"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:05.880
moment of sorts. The first official&nbsp;
LEGO sets featuring the hero were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:09.720
tie-ins for Sam Raimi's "Spider-Man,"&nbsp;
released in 2002. With that said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:13.740
the LEGO Spider-Man we see in "Across the&nbsp;
Spider-Verse" is based on the version that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:17.520
came with the comic-inspired "Daily&nbsp;
Bugle" set, which came out in 2021.

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:23.700
When Gwen Stacy is ordered to find and arrest&nbsp;
the Spot in Earth-50101's Mumbattan, an invisible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:29.280
Miles Morales secretly follows her. As a result,&nbsp;
Miles also meets Gwen's contact and Spider Society&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:33.960
teammate there: Pavitr Prabhakar, that Earth's&nbsp;
Spider-Man. Introduced as a lighthearted soul&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:38.640
who absolutely revels in being Spider-Man, Pavitr&nbsp;
gracefully swings through the city with his web&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:43.740
bangles, showing a natural gift for super heroics&nbsp;
despite being a superhero for just six months.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:50.160
"Being Spider-Man is so easy. I fight a few bad&nbsp;
guys, quick break for a chai with my auntie…"

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:53.400
He is also quick to sense the romantic&nbsp;
tension between Miles and Gwen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:56.220
and doesn't hesitate to bring it&nbsp;
up multiple times, while sharply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.220 --> 00:06:59.940
poking fun at Western stereotypes of Indian&nbsp;
culture during their fight against the Spot.

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:03.540
Unfortunately, this encounter paves&nbsp;
the way for a demonstration of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:07.260
consequences of multiversal meddling.&nbsp;
When Miles prevents a "canon event" by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:12.420
saving Earth-50101's Inspector Singh from&nbsp;
being crushed by debris, Pavitr's reality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:16.860
begins to collapse. We then see the Spider&nbsp;
Society's multiverse repair team on the scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:19.980
planting devices that were designed&nbsp;
to prevent dimensional incursions.

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:25.140
In the comics, the character first appeared&nbsp;
in 2005's "Spider-Man: India" #1. There,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:28.680
he sports a costume that is much closer in&nbsp;
appearance to the classic Spider-Man suit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:31.680
but with white pants and red&nbsp;
pointed shoes. Additionally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:35.280
the comics version of Pavitr operates in&nbsp;
Mumbai rather than a made-up location.

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:38.760
Whenever applicable, "Across the&nbsp;
Spider-Verse" makes sure that each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:43.080
Spider-character incorporates visual aspects of&nbsp;
their respective dimensions into their overall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:47.940
aesthetic. There is no better example of this&nbsp;
than the Spider-Man of Earth-138, Hobie Brown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:52.380
the Spider-Punk. A literal walking&nbsp;
collage of newspapers and band posters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:56.640
the anarchic Spider-Punk cooly strums his&nbsp;
guitar in the heat of battle, bringing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:01.440
electrifying energy wherever he goes while never&nbsp;
hesitating to give The Man the middle finger.

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:05.580
Hobie lends some much-needed assistance to&nbsp;
Spider-Gwen, Miles, and Pavitr Prabhakar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:10.380
at a critical moment on Earth-50101, as&nbsp;
they try to stop the Spot from activating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:15.000
Alchemax's super collider in that reality.&nbsp;
He also accompanies Miles and Gwen to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:20.520
Spider Society HQ on Earth-928, where it quickly&nbsp;
becomes clear that he and Miguel O'Hara mutually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:25.080
dislike each other. In one sequence, Hobie is&nbsp;
thrilled when Peter B. Parker's daughter Mayday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:29.520
fills her diaper while Miguel is holding her,&nbsp;
saluting the child while calling the act "taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:33.600
a crap on the establishment." Crucially,&nbsp;
Hobie helps Miles escape from captivity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:37.620
taking advantage of the ensuing chaos&nbsp;
at the Spider Society HQ to quit.

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:42.660
Later on, his friendship with Gwen becomes a&nbsp;
massive boon when both Miles and Gwen go rogue:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.660 --> 00:08:46.440
The "Project Bootleg" dimension-hopping&nbsp;
device he leaves for her on Earth-65&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:49.980
enables Gwen to recruit her trusted&nbsp;
Spider-allies to join her splinter group.

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:55.560
The Spider-Mobile is one of the oddest yet most&nbsp;
iconic parts of Spider-Man's comic book history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.560 --> 00:09:00.660
Built together by Spider-Man and his best bud, the&nbsp;
Human Torch, the Spider-Mobile made its first full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:06.960
appearance in 1974's "Amazing Spider-Man" #130&nbsp;
but was rendered unusable roughly 30 issues later.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:11.820
Still, it has been brought back repeatedly in the&nbsp;
books, as both a functional vehicle and a museum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:15.540
display piece, much to the web slinger's&nbsp;
embarrassment. In recent years, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:21.600
its most noteworthy comeback was in 2015's&nbsp;
"Amazing Spider-Man" Vol. 3 #12, not as a vehicle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:27.600
for Spider-Man to ride, but as a legitimate,&nbsp;
sentient Spider-Man variation from Earth-53931.

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:31.860
This version of the Spider-Mobile, along with&nbsp;
the secret identity of "Peter Parkedcar," makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:35.280
an appearance in "Spider-Man: Across&nbsp;
the Spider-Verse." It is perhaps the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:39.240
sheer ridiculousness of Parkedcar's existence&nbsp;
that earned it a few seconds in the spotlight:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:42.900
The Spider-Mobile is one of the few&nbsp;
Spider Society members Miles gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:46.380
officially introduced to, complete with&nbsp;
its Earth designation in a caption box,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:51.300
while he explores the facilities of the Spider&nbsp;
Society HQ with Spider-Gwen, Spider-Punk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:55.680
and Jessica Drew. Parkedcar also joins the rest&nbsp;
of the Society in trying to apprehend Miles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:59.820
but it humorously fails to cross the web&nbsp;
tightrope held by another Spider-variant.

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:02.040
One look at the Scarlet Spider in "Spider-Man:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:06.000
Across the Spider-Verse" would be enough for&nbsp;
any comics fan to know that this version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:10.500
Ben Reilly embodies the essence of grim&nbsp;
and gritty 1990s comics, from heavily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:14.700
inked head to toe. Unfortunately, fans who&nbsp;
were expecting a respectable interpretation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.700 --> 00:10:18.240
of the fan-favorite Spider-clone are&nbsp;
unlikely to find it in this iteration.

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:22.080
Hailing from Earth-94, a reference to&nbsp;
the character's first appearance as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:27.300
Scarlet Spider in 1994's "Web of Spider-Man"&nbsp;
#118, the Ben Reilly we meet in this movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:32.340
is a tongue-in-cheek caricature of '90s comic&nbsp;
book cliches. From his unnecessarily verbose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:36.420
descriptions of otherwise mundane objects,&nbsp;
his obsession with muscles and hitting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:41.100
"perfect pose," and his penchant for whiny&nbsp;
introspection, it's a Herculean challenge to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:45.180
take this character seriously, particularly&nbsp;
because he takes himself too seriously.

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:49.140
The Scarlet Spider joins Miguel O'Hara and&nbsp;
Jessica Drew during the film's third act as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:53.880
they search for Miles Morales on Earth-1610.&nbsp;
Unfortunately, his peculiar mannerisms and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:58.200
profound lack of awareness quickly become his&nbsp;
undoing. Spider-Gwen makes short work of him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:01.560
tricking him into getting sucked into&nbsp;
her bootleg portal while separating him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:05.640
from his dimension-crossing device. At this&nbsp;
point, it's anyone's guess if he'll show up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:10.020
in the next film, and if he'll be more than&nbsp;
just a walking stereotype played for laughs.

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:15.000
When Miles first steps into the Spider&nbsp;
Society HQ, he meets a mind-blowing array&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:19.260
of Spider-characters with different abilities.&nbsp;
Still, this doesn't stop the excited youth from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:23.220
introducing himself as "Spider-Man" to the&nbsp;
cyberspace Spider-hero called Spider-Byte,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:26.880
who sarcastically deadpans,&nbsp;
"Whoa, no way… All of us are."

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:30.540
In "Across the Spider-Verse," Margo's&nbsp;
digital avatar acts as the gatekeeper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:33.240
of sorts for the Spider Society.&nbsp;
Operating from the control room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:37.260
one of Spider-Byte's responsibilities is to&nbsp;
send the multiverse-crossing supervillains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.260 --> 00:11:41.580
the Society detains back to their respective&nbsp;
dimensions. As the resident arachnid-themed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:46.980
avenger of Earth-22191, this version of Margo&nbsp;
Kess could just as easily be another virtual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:51.840
extension of her comic book counterpart, who&nbsp;
first appeared in 2018's "Vault of Spiders" #1.

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:56.220
After successfully eluding the entire Spider&nbsp;
Society, Miles uses his ability to turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.220 --> 00:12:01.260
invisible to evade Margo's gaze and sneaks into&nbsp;
the teleporter in an attempt to send himself back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:06.120
to Earth-1610. However, because the teleporter&nbsp;
selects where to send its passenger based on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:11.460
origin of their powers, it mistakenly sends Miles&nbsp;
to Earth-42, the home dimension of the spider that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.460 --> 00:12:16.200
bit him. Crucially, Margo gets an opportunity to&nbsp;
reboot the system and prevent Miles from escaping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:21.660
but opts not to do anything. Instead, she lets&nbsp;
him beam out of their HQ. So it's no surprise when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:26.760
she shows up as a member of Spider-Gwen's newly&nbsp;
formed rogue faction in the film's final scenes.

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:30.300
Of course, it wouldn't be a proper&nbsp;
"Spider-Verse" movie without everyone's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:33.300
favorite easygoing Spider-sensei, Peter B. Parker.

00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:36.780
"Watch this. He's gonna say,&nbsp;
'You've got twenty-four hours."

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:38.280
"You've got twenty-four hours."

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:43.080
This time around, it looks like the&nbsp;
old, tired Spider-Man of Earth-616,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:47.640
or as Spider-Punk calls him, "Humbling Reality&nbsp;
Spider-Man," has managed to finally pick himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.640 --> 00:12:51.480
up and get his act together. He has also&nbsp;
traded his homeless, dimension-displaced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.480 --> 00:12:54.780
get-up from "Into the Spider-Verse" for&nbsp;
a proper set of threads, specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.780 --> 00:13:00.060
a pink bathrobe and baby harness. In other words,&nbsp;
the dadbod Spider-Man has become an actual dad.

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:03.960
Upon his return to his own dimension,&nbsp;
Peter reconciles with Mary Jane Watson.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:07.020
Their rekindled love results in the&nbsp;
birth of their daughter, Mayday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:10.980
who has inherited her father's amazing&nbsp;
spider powers. Ever the devoted parent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:15.840
Peter made her a web-shooting bracelet. As it&nbsp;
turns out, Peter's adventure with Miles Morales in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:19.920
the previous film helped him get over his fear of&nbsp;
becoming a parent. He even admits as much to his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:24.720
protégé during their brief but heated conversation&nbsp;
after Miles breaks out of the Spider Society HQ.

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:28.680
After Miles escapes, Peter returns to&nbsp;
his homeworld, setting Mayday in her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.680 --> 00:13:33.120
crib and halfheartedly denying to MJ that he&nbsp;
brought their kid on another fight. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:36.840
this quiet night at the Parker household&nbsp;
comes to an end when Spider-Gwen pops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:40.440
up in Peter's backyard through Hobie's&nbsp;
dimension-hopping device. As expected,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.440 --> 00:13:42.840
Peter decides to join Gwen&nbsp;
in her search for Miles.

00:13:43.500 --> 00:13:46.860
When the first trailer for "Spider-Man:&nbsp;
Across the Spider-Verse" was released,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.860 --> 00:13:51.120
eagle-eyed Spider-fans immediately spotted&nbsp;
a familiar, albeit largely forgotten,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:54.960
version of the web slinger mixed in with the&nbsp;
rest of the Spider Society: the Peter Parker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.960 --> 00:14:01.200
of Earth-751263, otherwise known as Spider-Man&nbsp;
Unlimited from the eponymous 1999 cartoon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:05.940
This small detail got many fans excited, as it&nbsp;
pretty much confirmed the likelihood of other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:09.600
more popular animated Spider-Men showing&nbsp;
up in "Across the Spider-Verse" as well.

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:14.160
True enough, subsequent promotional materials&nbsp;
revealed that the Spider-Man of the fan-favorite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:20.520
"Spectacular Spider-Man" animated series from&nbsp;
2008, designated as Earth-26496 is also part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.520 --> 00:14:24.540
of the movie. While Unlimited didn't really get to&nbsp;
do anything significant during the film's runtime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:28.800
Spectacular gets two moments to shine.&nbsp;
Aside from a scene during Miguel O'Hara's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:33.420
Arachno-Humanoid Poly-Multiverse explainer&nbsp;
depicting Spectacular's version of the ASM-90&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.420 --> 00:14:37.740
canon event, voice actor Josh Keaton&nbsp;
briefly reprises his role. He gets to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:42.120
deliver a one-liner meant to dissuade Miles&nbsp;
from preventing Earth-1610's canon event:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.120 --> 00:14:45.780
the death of his father, the recently&nbsp;
promoted Captain Jeff Morales.

00:14:45.780 --> 00:14:49.320
One not-so-surprising inclusion&nbsp;
is the Spider-Man from Earth-67,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.320 --> 00:14:54.060
the Spider-Man from the pointing meme. The '67&nbsp;
Spider-Man joins the Society in pursuing Miles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.060 --> 00:14:57.300
bravely announcing his arrival&nbsp;
before demonstrating his signature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.300 --> 00:15:00.660
ineffectiveness by swinging way too&nbsp;
slow to catch the renegade Spider.

00:15:00.660 --> 00:15:04.500
It's safe to say that one of the&nbsp;
biggest questions on everyone's minds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:07.800
about "Across the Spider-Verse" was:&nbsp;
would Tobey Maguire, Andrew Garfield,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:12.540
and Tom Holland be part of the movie? After all,&nbsp;
we recently witnessed the live-action Spider-trio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.540 --> 00:15:17.100
make cinematic history in 2021's "Spider-Man:&nbsp;
No Way Home," so it would make sense for them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.100 --> 00:15:20.880
to be in another multiverse-spanning&nbsp;
Spider-flick. The answer is…sort of.

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:25.680
Both Maguire, the Friendly Neighborhood&nbsp;
Spider-Man of Earth-96283= and Garfield,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:31.440
the Amazing Spider-Man of Earth-120703 play&nbsp;
small roles in "Across the Spider-Verse."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:35.760
Miguel explains to Miles that every Spider-Man&nbsp;
must go through a canon event that involves a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.760 --> 00:15:39.000
character close to the web slinger of&nbsp;
the universe dying in a tragic way.

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:44.220
During the explanation, a few seconds of the death&nbsp;
of Capt. George Stacy from 2012's "The Amazing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.220 --> 00:15:48.900
Spider-Man" is shown. Moments later, the death&nbsp;
of Uncle Ben, arguably the canon event of canon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:53.220
events for most versions of Spider-Man, becomes&nbsp;
the focus of the discussion. In the background,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.220 --> 00:15:57.900
we see snippets of Maguire and Cliff Robertson's&nbsp;
Ben Parker, who dies in 2002's "Spider-Man."

00:15:57.900 --> 00:16:01.380
Meanwhile, Holland's Spider-Man&nbsp;
doesn't appear at all, although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.380 --> 00:16:04.260
he does get mentioned in the first few&nbsp;
minutes of "Across the Spider-Verse."

00:16:04.260 --> 00:16:09.060
"Don't even get me started on Doctor Strange&nbsp;
and the little nerd from Earth-199999."

00:16:09.720 --> 00:16:13.260
While many of the members of the Spider&nbsp;
Society in "Across the Spider-Verse" can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.260 --> 00:16:16.260
easily be described as Spider-Man&nbsp;
variants based on their costumes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.260 --> 00:16:20.580
there are a handful with distinct visual elements&nbsp;
that make them stand out from the Spider-crowd.

00:16:20.580 --> 00:16:24.360
Undoubtedly, one of them is Max&nbsp;
Borne, the Spider-Man of 2211,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.360 --> 00:16:28.860
who hails from the comic book world of&nbsp;
Earth-9500. This futuristic Spider-Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.860 --> 00:16:33.180
takes the arachnid aesthetic to a whole new&nbsp;
level. Aside from his multi-eyed helmet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.180 --> 00:16:37.380
he also sports four red appendages protruding&nbsp;
from what looks like a metallic backpack.

00:16:37.380 --> 00:16:42.240
In "Across the Spider-Verse," Spider-Man 2211&nbsp;
briefly steals the show near the beginning of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:46.440
Miles Morales' extended chase sequence,&nbsp;
when the teenager hilariously clings to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.440 --> 00:16:49.860
Borne's crimson carapace in a feeble&nbsp;
attempt to hide from the rest of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.860 --> 00:16:54.060
Society. This also causes the Society to&nbsp;
mistake Borne for Miles for a few seconds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.060 --> 00:16:58.380
emphasizing how the group has become so large&nbsp;
that they don't even know one another's names.

00:16:58.380 --> 00:17:03.360
Interestingly enough, Spider-Man 2211's first&nbsp;
appearance was in a comic book that featured two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:08.280
different versions of the web slinger meeting: the&nbsp;
1995 one-shot "Spider-Man 2099 Meets Spider-Man"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.280 --> 00:17:14.100
#1. Similar to his role in this film, Spider-Man&nbsp;
2211's job in that comic is to help preserve the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.100 --> 00:17:17.880
integrity of the timeline. He appears in the&nbsp;
nick of time to save Peter Parker and Miguel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.880 --> 00:17:23.340
O'Hara from being erased from existence via&nbsp;
the "retcon bombs" of the Hobgoblin of 2211.

00:17:23.340 --> 00:17:26.340
As part of the marketing efforts&nbsp;
for "Across the Spider-Verse," Sony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.340 --> 00:17:30.000
released a clip welcoming audiences&nbsp;
to the "Spider-Zoo." Indeed, there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:34.320
a mini-menagerie of Spider-powered animals who&nbsp;
somehow qualified for Spider Society membership.

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:38.760
Among the first we meet is Widow, nicknamed&nbsp;
"Spider-Horse," the trusty masked steed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.760 --> 00:17:43.800
of Patrick O'Hara, the cowboy Spider-Man&nbsp;
of Earth-31913. There's also Spider-Cat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.800 --> 00:17:46.440
a character originally from 2011's "Spider-Island:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.440 --> 00:17:51.360
I Love New York City" #1, who spits up&nbsp;
a web-hairball in Miles Morales' face.

00:17:51.360 --> 00:17:54.540
Spider-Monkey from the Marvel&nbsp;
Apes dimension also appears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.540 --> 00:17:57.660
a few times in the extended chase&nbsp;
sequence, though the spider-powered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.660 --> 00:18:00.960
simian's agility does little good in&nbsp;
helping the Society apprehend Miles.

00:18:00.960 --> 00:18:03.840
The most attention-grabbing&nbsp;
Spider-creature here, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:09.480
is Spider-Rex, a Tyrannosaurus rex in a Spider-Man&nbsp;
costume, complete with web-shooters around its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.480 --> 00:18:14.580
tiny wrists. A recent comics creation that&nbsp;
debuted in 2022's "Edge of Spider-Verse" #1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.580 --> 00:18:19.260
Spider-Rex was originally a Pteranodon&nbsp;
simultaneously bitten by a T. rex and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.260 --> 00:18:24.360
smashed by a meteorite with alien spider&nbsp;
stowaways. A "Freaky Friday" situation ensues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.360 --> 00:18:29.340
with the Pteranodon now inhabiting the T. rex's&nbsp;
body, inexplicably clad in a spider-themed suit.

00:18:29.340 --> 00:18:31.500
"It CAN get weirder!"

00:18:31.500 --> 00:18:37.020
Nicolas Cage told Screen Rant in January 2023&nbsp;
that Sony did not reach out to him to reprise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.020 --> 00:18:41.040
his role as Spider-Man Noir in "Into the&nbsp;
Spider-Verse." Neither Kimiko Glenn nor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:45.060
John Mulaney, who played Peni Parker and&nbsp;
Spider-Ham, confirmed their participation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.060 --> 00:18:49.080
quashing hopes for the secondary Spider-trio’s&nbsp;
return in "Across the Spider-Verse."

00:18:49.080 --> 00:18:53.820
As it turns out, however, the multiversal&nbsp;
pals do pop up unexpectedly — and it looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.820 --> 00:18:57.360
like they'll play even bigger roles in&nbsp;
the next film, "Beyond the Spider-Verse."

00:18:57.360 --> 00:19:00.300
The first of the three to appear in&nbsp;
"Across the Spider-Verse" is Peni,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.300 --> 00:19:05.040
who is seen piloting an upgraded, more streamlined&nbsp;
version of her companion spider's robotic costume,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.040 --> 00:19:09.240
after the original was destroyed in the previous&nbsp;
movie's final battle. This new mechanical suit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:09.240 --> 00:19:14.040
closely resembles its comic book counterpart,&nbsp;
as seen in 2014's "Edge of Spider-Verse" #5.

00:19:14.040 --> 00:19:17.700
As for Spider-Ham and Spider-Man Noir,&nbsp;
the two show up at the end of the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.700 --> 00:19:20.100
as members of Spider-Gwen's splinter Spider-squad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.100 --> 00:19:24.540
to help her outmaneuver Miguel O'Hara's&nbsp;
Spider Society and find Miles first.

00:19:24.540 --> 00:19:28.200
Miguel O'Hara's Spider Society spans&nbsp;
the entirety of the Spider-Verse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:32.940
and features both existing characters and original&nbsp;
heroes created for "Across the Spider-Verse."

00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:36.840
A good number of the Spider Society members&nbsp;
draw design inspiration from established&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.840 --> 00:19:41.460
Spider-folks but aren't 100% accurate to&nbsp;
their multimedia counterparts. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.460 --> 00:19:43.560
one character looks like Arachnido Jr.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.560 --> 00:19:48.000
while a few are wearing different versions of&nbsp;
Earth-616 Spider-Man's various Spider-Armors.

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:53.160
One Society member resembles Spider-Man's brutal&nbsp;
clone, Spidercide; others draw inspiration from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:57.060
characters like Ai Apaec and Julia&nbsp;
Carpenter's Spider-Woman. Some fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:57.060 --> 00:20:01.260
even reportedly spotted Takuya Yamashiro,&nbsp;
Toei's live-action Spider-Man, in the mix.

00:20:01.260 --> 00:20:05.700
We also see Spinneret and Spiderling,&nbsp;
Mangaverse Spider-Man from Earth-2301,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:05.700 --> 00:20:09.660
and a version of the Bombastic Bagman.&nbsp;
Curiously, the Spider-Man from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.660 --> 00:20:15.060
Insomniac game universe appears not once,&nbsp;
but twice: one wearing his PS4 Advanced Suit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.060 --> 00:20:20.100
and the other rocking the suit from "Marvel's&nbsp;
Spider-Man 2" for the PS5. And as expected,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.100 --> 00:20:23.100
many of these characters don't really&nbsp;
do anything significant in the film.

00:20:23.100 --> 00:20:26.580
Meanwhile, the Spider-variants created&nbsp;
for "Across the Spider-Verse" include&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.580 --> 00:20:31.200
Malala Windsor (aka Spider-UK of&nbsp;
Earth-835), Cyborg Spider-Woman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:31.200 --> 00:20:35.100
and the Spidersona of Metro Boomin, who worked&nbsp;
on the "Across the Spider-Verse" soundtrack.

